          %h2#introduction Introduction
          %p
            In this article, we collect some results about representable functors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(c, -) \colon C \to V
            (where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            is some base of hom-enrichment) that preserve coproducts.
          %p
            When
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensive+category"} extensive category
            regarded as enriched in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            a
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+object"} connected object
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo =
                    %mi Top
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C = Top
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            the category of graphs, etc. Some basic results with proofs may be found at
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+object"} connected object
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A connected colimit (i.e., a colimit over a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+category"} connected diagram
                of) connected objects is connected.
            %li
              %p
                If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                is connected and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to Y
                is epic, then
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
                is connected.
          %p
            For non-extensive categories (e.g., categories of modules), the relation to “connectedness” tends to be less intuitive
            %sup#fnref:1>
              %a{:href => "#fn:1", :rel => "footnote"} 1
            \. Nevertheless, the concept of (arbitrary) coproduct-preserving representable remains important, and it is useful to collect some basic information.
          %h2#absoluteness Absoluteness
          %p
            In an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ab-enriched+category"} Ab-enriched category
            %em finite
            coproducts are
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/absolute+colimits"} absolute colimits
            %em every
            representable. Thus, the interest is in representables which preserve infinite coproducts as well (which is what most of this page is about).
          %p
            At the other extreme from extensive categories, in a
            = succeed "-enriched" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SupLat"} SupLat
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SupLat"} SupLat
            or
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rel"} Rel
            %em arbitrary
            coproducts are absolute, and hence preserved by every representable.
          %h2#module_categories Module categories
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Mod
                    %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Mod}_R
            denote the category of right modules over a ring
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            induces a coproduct-preserving hom-functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi Mod
                      %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi Mod
                      %mi R
                    %mo →
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Ab
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Mod}_R(M, -) \colon \mathbf{Mod}_R \to \mathbf{Ab}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            appear in the literature, “compact” and “dually slender” among them. In any event, a
            %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/questions/59282/sums-compact-objects-f-g-objects-in-categories-of-modules"} question arose on MathOverflow
            as to whether or to what extent this condition coincides with the condition of being finitely generated, for various rings
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
          %p
            The information provided below was mostly culled from the answers given to that question, especially those by
            %a{:href => "#Gaillard"} Pierre-Yves Gaillard
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Muro"} Fernando Muro
          %h3#positive_results Positive results
          %p
            We begin with some easy preliminary remarks. Given a family of objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{B_i\}_{i \in I}
            in an
            = succeed "-enriched" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Ab
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Ab}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and a functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Ab
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \colon C \to \mathbf{Ab}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊕
                    %mi i
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊕
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \oplus_i F(B_i) \to F(\oplus_i B_i)
          %p
            and if this arrow is an isomorphism for every family
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_i
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            preserves coproducts. Turning to the case of representable functors on modules, let
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi j
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊕
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mi i
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_j \colon \oplus_i B_i \to B_j
          %p
            be the obvious projection (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi j
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mi j
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi B
                        %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_j \circ i_j = 1_{B_j}
            , else
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi j
                    %mo ∘
                    %msub
                      %mi i
                      %mi k
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_j \circ i_k = 0
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi M
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mo ⊕
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon M \to \oplus_i B_i
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi j
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi j
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi f
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_j \coloneqq p_j \circ f.
          %p
            Then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Mod
                    %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Mod}_R(M, -)
            preserves the particular coproduct
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊕
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \oplus_i B_i
            if for each
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi M
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mo ⊕
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon M \to \oplus_i B_i
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi j
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_j = 0
            for all but finitely many
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
          %p
            Clearly
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Mod
                    %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Mod}_R(R, -)
            preserves coproducts, and if
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            are coproduct-preserving functors
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi Mod
                      %mi R
                    %mo →
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Ab
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Mod}_R \to \mathbf{Ab}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo ⊕
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \oplus G
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi Mod
                      %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi R
                      %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Mod}_R(R^n, -)
              preserves coproducts.
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi Mod
                      %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Mod}_R(M, -)
              preserves coproducts and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi M
                    %mo →
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q \colon M \to N
              is epic, then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi Mod
                      %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi N
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Mod}_R(N, -)
              preserves coproducts.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              Given
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi N
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mo ⊕
                        %mi i
                      %msub
                        %mi B
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon N \to \oplus_i B_i
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi j
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi q
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_j \circ f \circ q = 0
              for all but finitely many
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi j
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi j
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi f
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_j = p_j \circ f = 0
              for all but finitely many
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
              since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
              is epic.
          %p Combining the two preceding observations, we infer that
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi Mod
                      %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Mod}_R(M, -)
              preserves coproducts if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              is finitely generated.
          %p Here is a sharper description of coproduct-preserving representables, based on subobject lattices.
          #prop1.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi Mod
                      %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Mod}_R(M, -)
              preserves coproducts if and only if the union of every countable chain of proper submodules of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              is a proper submodule.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              (As adapted from
              = succeed ".)" do
                %a{:href => "#Gaillard"} Gaillard’s answer
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi M
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ⊂
                    %msub
                      %mi M
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi ⋯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_0\subset M_1\subset\cdots
              be a chain of proper submodules of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              whose union is
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Q
                        %mi n
                      %mo =
                      %mi M
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %msub
                        %mi M
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q_n = M/M_n
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m \in M
              we have
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi q
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi m
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q_n(m) = 0
              for all but finitely many
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %msub
                      %mi q
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi M
                    %mo →
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mi n
                    %msub
                      %mi Q
                      %mi n
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q = \langle q_n \rangle \colon M \to \prod_n Q_n,
            %p
              corresponding to the tuple of quotient maps
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi q
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi M
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi Q
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q_n \colon M \to Q_n
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ⊕
                        %mi n
                      %msub
                        %mi Q
                        %mi n
                      %mo ↪
                      %msub
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                        %mi n
                      %msub
                        %mi Q
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \oplus_n Q_n \hookrightarrow \prod_n Q_n
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi q
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q_n
              is nonzero,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
              does not belong to the subgroup
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ⊕
                      %mi n
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi Mod
                      %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi Q
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↪
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mi n
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi Mod
                      %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi Q
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≅
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi Mod
                      %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo ,
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mi n
                    %msub
                      %mi Q
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \oplus_n \mathbf{Mod}_R(M, Q_n) \hookrightarrow \prod_n \mathbf{Mod}_R(M, Q_n) \cong \mathbf{Mod}_R(M, \prod_n Q_n)
            %p
              and thus the canonical map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ⊕
                      %mi n
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi Mod
                      %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi Q
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi Mod
                      %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mo ⊕
                      %mi n
                    %msub
                      %mi Q
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \oplus_n \mathbf{Mod}_R(M, Q_n) \to \mathbf{Mod}_R(M, \oplus_n Q_n)
              is not an isomorphism.
            %p
              In the other direction, if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi Mod
                      %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
